<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vseslav of Polotsk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1039–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vseslav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> was prince of Polotsk from 1044 to 1101 and a rival of the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>. Defeated on the Nemiga river near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in 1067, he was seized during negotiations and imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, but the Kievans freed him in 1068 and briefly made him their prince. He built St Sophia in Polotsk, and after his death his sons divided his lands into Polotsk, Minsk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a> and other principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk.jpg" alt="Vseslav of Polotsk" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pirags · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D1%81%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%91%D1%80%D1%8F%D1%87%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D1%8F%D1%8E%D1%82_%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B7_%D0%94%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vseslav Bryachislavich was the son of Bryachislav Izyaslavich, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, and so a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogneda-of-polotsk">Rogneda of Polotsk</a>. He took the throne of Polotsk in 1044 on his father's death and married around 1060. Because his father had never been prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, he was excluded from the grand princely succession as an izgoi. As the only major prince in Rus not descended from Yaroslav, he was, in the words of Simon Franklin and Jonathan Shepard, an outsider from within.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Unable to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, which Yaroslav's three sons held, he began to pillage the northern lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>. In 1065 he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> but was thrown back, and in the winter of 1066 to 1067 he pillaged and burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod the Great</a>, taking the bell and other objects from its Cathedral of Holy Wisdom to decorate his own cathedral of the same name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>. The attack forced the young Mstislav to flee from Novgorod to his father Iziaslav in Kiev. The Yaroslavichi joined forces, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and defeated Vseslav on the Nemiga River on 3 March 1067.</p><p>In June he was captured during peace talks, when Iziaslav broke his oath, and was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. In the Kiev Uprising of 1068, after a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> on the Alta River, the crowd freed Vseslav from prison and proclaimed him grand prince of Kiev, and Iziaslav fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Iziaslav returned with an army after seven months and retook the throne, and Vseslav fled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>. After several years of struggle he secured Polotsk in 1071. In his last thirty years his chief enemies were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-i-of-kiev">Vsevolod Yaroslavich</a> and Vsevolod's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vseslav died on 24 April 1101, the Wednesday before Good Friday according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, and was buried in the Cathedral of Holy Wisdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, the oldest stone building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. He had six sons, among them Gleb, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, Davyd, Prince of Polotsk, and Sviatoslav, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>. He had a reputation for sorcery: the Primary Chronicle says that he was conceived by sorcery and born with a caul on his head. The 12th-century Tale of Igor's Campaign depicts him as a werewolf who rode like a wolf from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to Tmutarakan and heard the bells of Polotsk from Kiev.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vseslav of Polotsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A prince could also lose his lands: by 1130 Mstislav the Great had exiled all the descendants of <b>Vseslav of Polotsk</b> to the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li><li><blockquote>Minsk first appears in the chronicle in 1067, when the sons of Yaroslav the Wise sacked it during their war with <b>Vseslav of Polotsk</b> and defeated him on the Nemiga river.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minsk">Principality of Minsk</a></li><li><blockquote>Its princes often fought the rest of Rus', and <b>Vseslav</b>, called the Sorcerer, who ruled for over fifty years, even held Kiev briefly in 1068.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-polotsk">Principality of Polotsk</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Vseslav of Polotsk</b> in 1101, his sons divided his lands, and Vitebsk became the seat of its own line of princes, though the details of its early rulers are poorly recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-vitebsk">Principality of Vitebsk</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polatsak</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polatsak</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.48,28.8&amp;year=1079" aria-label="Polatsak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5179 270 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vseslav of Polotsk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5629 540h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5629 540h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.48,28.8&amp;year=1079">What was here in 1079? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
